{"title":"Descriptive statistics and visualization","markdown":{"headingText":"Descriptive statistics and visualization","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-descriptive","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n\nDescriptive statistics form the first level of data analysis, focused on organizing, summarizing, and presenting data in a meaningful way. At this stage, the objective is not to draw conclusions beyond the observed dataset, but to understand its internal structure, distribution, and patterns. Raw data, in its original form, are often unstructured and difficult to interpret; descriptive methods convert these into interpretable summaries through tabulation, numerical measures, and graphical representation.\n\nThe process begins with data preparation, including cleaning, coding, and organizing variables into a usable format. Once structured, the data are summarized using measures of central tendency such as mean, median, and mode, which represent the typical value, and measures of dispersion such as standard deviation, interquartile range, and range, which reflect variability. These numerical summaries are complemented by visual tools—such as histograms, bar charts, and boxplots—that provide an intuitive understanding of distributional characteristics, outliers, and group differences.\n\nDescriptive statistics are essential for identifying data quality issues, detecting anomalies, and guiding subsequent analytical decisions. They provide the necessary context for inferential analysis by clarifying the nature of the data, including scale, distribution, and underlying patterns. Within Jamovi, these procedures can be executed efficiently through a graphical interface; however, their value lies in correct interpretation rather than mechanical computation. Descriptive analysis does not establish causation or generalize findings to a population, but it defines the foundation upon which all further statistical inference is built.\n\n\n\n::: {#fig-descriptive-analysis fig-cap=\"Framework of descriptive statistics\"}\n```{mermaid}\nflowchart TD\nA[Raw data]\nB[Data preparation<br/>Cleaning and coding]\nC[Organized data]\nD[Descriptive statistics]\nE[Central tendency<br/>Mean, median]\nF[Dispersion<br/>SD, IQR]\nG[Visualization<br/>Plots]\nH[Summary<br/>Patterns]\n\nA --> B\nB --> C\nC --> D\nD --> E\nD --> F\nD --> G\nE --> H\nF --> H\nG --> H\n```\n:::\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"monashthesis-html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"monashthesis-html","base-format":"html","extension-name":"monashthesis"},"execute":{"fig-width":8,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":false,"output":true,"warning":false,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":true,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"self-contained-math":false,"format-resources":["_extensions/monashthesis/fonts.html"],"notebook-links":true,"shortcodes":[]},"pandoc":{"standalone":true,"wrap":"none","default-image-extension":"png","to":"html","toc-depth":2,"number-sections":true,"html-math-method":"katex","include-in-header":{"file":"_exten